amples  we don't have to pry a primary tube out  of the cobalt hologic system  and everything is nicely organized in a  barcoded plate  those condensed positive plates then  make their way into the whole genome  sequencing process  i'll get into this in a little bit more  detail but the whole genome process  converts the entire  viral genome into something that we can  detect using a genome sequencer  to then identify the specific versions  of the sars cov2 virus present in each  positive sample  and then we associate those viruses with  the position that they fall in within  the viral family tree  the sequencing protocol is one that we  co-developed over many months in  collaboration with pacific biosciences  it's  loosely based off of the protocol mickey  free developed for oxford and  the oxford nanocore system and you can  see the citation for that paper  i'm here in the bottom right hand corner  but  the enzymes and primer concentrations of  that protocol  were optimized for pack bio along with  some modifications to the barcodes to  make them  uh amenable to the circular consensus  sequencing that's used  in the hi-fi platform for pacific  biosciences  the protocol itself is very  straightforward we start out  by creating cdna from residual pcr  testing material  amplify that material using two pools of  non-overlapping m13 tailed  primer pairs those pools are then  asymmetrically barcoded using m13 tags  and pooled for smart bell preparation  sequencing then proceeds on the sql 2  using a 20 hour movie for the data  collection portion  and this process has been fully  automated on  hamilton liquid handlers and we have  released the exact protocol that we use  for doing this on the pack bio website  so  if you're interested in running this  protocol on your own sql or sql 2  please stop by the pacific biosciences  website and you'll be able to  download the protocol there hi-fi yield  on the sql 2 has been  really impressive we're able to load to  a p1 of 70  