 insert size of short read approaches and  here you can see deletions in two  samples that would have been mostly  missed by  the short read single tile amplicon  approach  the pipeline that we've set up for  processing is also pretty standardized  we take the pac-bio-generated ccs reads  into alignment and variant calling  for this we use clc but there are other  tools like minimap and juliet that  work just as well one of the major  problems that we ran into with  generating consensus sequences  was that there really weren't any great  tools for doing this that didn't  introduce frame shifts into the  consensus  so once again we co-developed a method  with pac bio to solve this problem  and this is work that was spearheaded  mostly by liz  sang at pac bio along with lax ire and  chen zhang at labcorp who developed a  program called the vcf cons  to generate these consensus sequences  from a vcf file based on  read depth by replacing missing sequence  in the reference with ends and  inserting variants with greater than 50  frequency into the reference  and this completely eliminated the frame  shift effect that we saw with the  the more traditional uh methods for  performing consensus sequence generation  vcf cons has been  published and it's a another joint  pre-print from  pac biome lab corp and the code for it  is also available on github  for the rest of the pipeline clade calls  are made with next glade lineage calls  are made with pangolin  and all of the raw data and metadata and  consensus information is assembled and  packaged for delivery to the cdc  but that pipeline wasn't generated  overnight and the genome sequencing work  originally started  as an internal research and development  project we recognized early on  that we were going to have a centralized  location for collecting positive samples  and began as we  as soon as we possibly could to save  these samples in the r d freezers  and it was clear to us that no one had  the same access to positive samples that  we did  and that we could play a really critical  role in understanding how the pandemic  began  so every day we would go into the lab  and steal all of the plates and primary  tubes that were going to be thrown away  and  at the end of the day we picked out all  of the  the positive samples for archive we did  all of this at the same time that we  were  developing the pcr testing  infrastructure that is  now used to support over half of the  sars cov2 testing at labcorp  and we spent most of the summer  developing the sequencing protocol in  our  spare time and then we sequenced 10 000  of these early  positive samples between september and  november of 2020.  on december 7th dr michael levandoski  presented the results  of the early sequencing work at the asm  microbial ngs conference  and then in that presentation he  highlighted this model that we had  developed for doing national genomic  surveillance and on december 27th  we were awarded a cdc contract to  continue doing this work on a national  level  in real time we were awarded that  contract because of the data we had  generated  here you can see the sars kobe 2 genome  on the bottom of the graph and the bars  you see growing  and the animation are the number of  mutations we detected at those positions  of the genome of the virus over the  course of our surveillance study  these mutations matter a lot with  respect to how the virus functions and  its ability to infect the host  evade the immune system or even thought  treatments that  we've developed to combat it and i want  to draw your  attention to one mutation in particular  we've all heard about spike protein  mutations and  here i'm highlighting the d614g mutation  everyone  almost call probably knows that this is  an important variant but it became the  most important variant in the us  much sooner than most people realize and  that's because it spread across the  country  during a time when there was very little  surveillance going 