n tasked with  sequencing a subset of those thousands  as a part of  cdc's baseline study this allows us to  help the cdc track the  individual viruses they spread across  the u.s and those results are presented  on  the weekly cdc spheres calls to do this  we  centrally collect thousands of sample  plates a week in north carolina condense  them into the positive plates  and sequence the genomes of those  randomly selected positive samples  but what what does this look like in  practice um  this map here um this animated map shows  where the  positives we're picking come from and  the intensity of the states represent  how many positives  we pulled from those states relative to  the others and  the animation ends on the cumulative  total  that we've sequenced so far within  the cdc project  we can also generate clay doughnut  charts to show how the prevalence of  each clade has or hasn't changed in the  entire data set  at the state level obviously there are  some sample size effects here but it's  still interesting  to visualize and a few interesting  things have appeared in the last few  months especially relating to trends of  the variance of concern in some states  and a good example of this is the  evolution of the clade distribution in  florida  which is having a significant outbreak  of the b.117 variant also known  and listed here as 20i 501y  v1 you can see that variant approaching  about  25 of the samples that we see every week  out of florida  but you might be asking yourself what  the work we've done has actually made an  impact and  i think the answer to that question  would be yes it was a lab corp  sequencing surveillance efforts that  detected the first case of the south  african variant in the u.s  proving that we're able to detect things  when we look for them and  we can have an impact on our ability to  combat the virus by  providing information to state and  federal teams as they respond  to the emergence of these new variants  and this isn't t