y have built  up a reasonable  amount of population immunity and it  appears that  this virus doesn't really care about  that um  but one of the most important aspects of  the  labcorp data set is that it's based on  random sampling unlike other sequencing  initiatives that target sequencing  samples that they believe are variants  of concern we sequence samples randomly  across the nation  this limits the level of sampling bias  in our data set although there  are still states that we end up sampling  more than others  but generally this data set is much less  targeted than other initiatives  because of this we can look at national  trends and the distribution of clades  across the country  the dominant clade throughout our  sampling has been 20g  which is the major u.s clade that was  described early on in the pandemic  in the united states but as you can see  here in the last few weeks the  prevalence of 20g  has been reduced while 20i501y.v1 which  is b.117  and clade 20b are seeing a bit of an  uptick  indicating that the other two clades may  be out competing 20g  potentially a little or too early to to  say that but that that looks like that's  where the the  the trend is going um and this trend um  for b117 is  also in agreement with early predictions  that this variant would become the  major u.s variant by by the beginning to  the end of march  so i hope that this presentation has  helped convince you  of the importance of genomic  surveillance during a pandemic  sequencing based surveillance allows us  to know the things that we don't know  uh we've deposited over 15 000 sequences  and you say it over the  past nine weeks in collaboration with  cdc combined with the work of other  state labs and universities  approximately 10 000 new sequences are  making it into the databases every week  and and with that we're finally now  getting the data and the tools we need  to respond to this pandemic with a  reasoned scientific approach and here  i'm showing a  a 