amples  we don't have to pry a primary tube out  of the cobalt hologic system  and everything is nicely organized in a  barcoded plate  those condensed positive plates then  make their way into the whole genome  sequencing process  i'll get into this in a little bit more  detail but the whole genome process  converts the entire  viral genome into something that we can  detect using a genome sequencer  to then identify the specific versions  of the sars cov2 virus present in each  positive sample  and then we associate those viruses with  the position that they fall in within  the viral family tree  the sequencing protocol is one that we  co-developed over many months in  collaboration with pacific biosciences  it's  loosely based off of the protocol mickey  free developed for oxford and  the oxford nanocore system and you can  see the citation for that paper  i'm here in the bottom right hand corner  but  the enzymes and primer concentrations of  that protocol  were optimized for pack bio along with  some modifications to the barcodes to  make them  uh amenable to the circular consensus  sequencing that's used  in the hi-fi platform for pacific  biosciences  the protocol itself is very  straightforward we start out  by creating cdna from residual pcr  testing material  amplify that material using two pools of  non-overlapping m13 tailed  primer pairs those pools are then  asymmetrically barcoded using m13 tags  and pooled for smart bell preparation  sequencing then proceeds on the sql 2  using a 20 hour movie for the data  collection portion  and this process has been fully  automated on  hamilton liquid handlers and we have  released the exact protocol that we use  for doing this on the pack bio website  so  if you're interested in running this  protocol on your own sql or sql 2  please stop by the pacific biosciences  website and you'll be able to  download the protocol there hi-fi yield  on the sql 2 has been  really impressive we're able to load to  a p1 of 70  without failing runs with a mean read  length of thirty thousand  or thirty seven thousand uh faces and a  mean  sub read length of thirty six hundred  bases  um we're seeing um loading very  coming across very consistently in the  60 to 70 percent range which  um helps a lot when we're putting about  800 samples on each run  using this method we can get whole  genome coverage from approximately 60  of the samples with a ct of less than 27  or we can get lineage coverage from  about 80 percent of the samples less  than 30 ct  that's really the only major trade-off  between pack bio and short read  approaches to get these long reads  you need high quality starting material  i've also included an  example here of the run metrics with a  waterfall plot to show  where we see performance drop off and  and highlight that for surveillance we  are sequencing samples  up to a ct of 30. but performance  appears to  seriously degrade around 27 ct  there are a number of optimizations that  are in the pipeline to try to recover  data from these samples above 27 ct  however the majority  of this effect is is due to the  availability of target capable of  producing long raids  for sequencing on the sql 2. so  we do have a new protocol on development  to increase  yield from these higher ct samples  utilizing  600 base pair reads and  preliminary results with that protocol  are very encouraging  but we can't talk about biosequencing  without  highlighting the advantages of long  reads  in variant detection and phasing and as  you can see here the  1200 base pair amplicons that we're  using allow us to phase variance within  samples where coverage is high enough to  do so  um this could be used in the future to  identify  interesting low frequency subtypes that  occur within individuals and potentially  discover  early novel variant strains as they  emerge  in addition to that minor varying and  phasing  we can also use long reads to capture  large deletions that exceed the maximum  insert size of short read approaches and  here you can see deletions in two  samples that would have been mostly  missed by  the short read single tile amplicon  approach  the pipeline that we've set up for  processing is also pretty standardized  we take the pac-bio-generated ccs reads  into alignment and variant calling  for this we use clc but there are other  tools like minimap and juliet that  work just as well one of the major  problems that we ran into with  generating consensus sequences  was that there really weren't any great  tools for doing this that didn't  introduce frame shifts into the  consensus  so once again we co-developed a method  with pac bio to solve this problem  and this is work that was spearheaded  mostly by liz  sang at pac bio along with lax ire and  chen zhang at labcorp who developed a  program called the vcf cons  to generate these consensus sequences  from a vcf file based on  read depth by replacing missing sequence  in the reference with ends and  inserting variants with greater than 50  frequency into the reference  and this completely eliminated the frame  shift effect that we saw with the  the more traditional uh methods for  performing consensus sequence generation  vcf cons has been  published and it's a another joint  pre-print from  pac biome lab corp and the code for it  is also available on github  for the rest of the pipeline clade calls  are made with next glade lineage calls  are made with pangolin  and all of the raw data and metadata and  consensus information is assembled and  packaged for delivery to the cdc  but that pipeline wasn't generated  overnight and the genome sequencing work  originally started  as an internal research and development  project we recognized early on  that we were going to have a centralized  location for collecting positive samples  and began as we  as soon as we possibly could to save  these samples in the r d freezers  and it was clear to us that no one had  the same access to positive samples that  we did  and that we could play a really critical  role in understanding how the pandemic  began  so every day we would go into the lab  and steal all of the plates and primary  tubes that were going to be thrown away  and  at the end of the day we picked out all  of the  the positive samples for archive we did  all of this at the same time that we  were  developing the pcr testing  infrastructure that is  now used to support over half of the  sars cov2 testing at labcorp  and we spent most of the summer  developing the sequencing protocol in  our  spare time and then we sequenced 10 000  of these early  positive samples between september and  november of 2020.  on december 7th dr michael levandoski  presented the results  of the early sequencing work at the asm  microbial ngs conference  and then in that presentation he  highlighted this model that we had  developed for doing national genomic  surveillance and on december 27th  we were awarded a cdc contract to  continue doing this work on a national  level  in real time we were awarded that  contract because of the data we had  generated  here you can see the sars kobe 2 genome  on the bottom of the graph and the bars  you see growing  and the animation are the number of  mutations we detected at those positions  of the genome of the virus over the  course of our surveillance study  these mutations matter a lot with  respect to how the virus functions and  its ability to infect the host  evade the immune system or even thought  treatments that  we've developed to combat it and i want  to draw your  attention to one mutation in particular  we've all heard about spike protein  mutations and  here i'm highlighting the d614g mutation  everyone  almost call probably knows that this is  an important variant but it became the  most important variant in the us  much sooner than most people realize and  that's because it spread across the  country  during a time when there was very little  surveillance going 