n tasked with  sequencing a subset of those thousands  as a part of  cdc's baseline study this allows us to  help the cdc track the  individual viruses they spread across  the u.s and those results are presented  on  the weekly cdc spheres calls to do this  we  centrally collect thousands of sample  plates a week in north carolina condense  them into the positive plates  and sequence the genomes of those  randomly selected positive samples  but what what does this look like in  practice um  this map here um this animated map shows  where the  positives we're picking come from and  the intensity of the states represent  how many positives  we pulled from those states relative to  the others and  the animation ends on the cumulative  total  that we've sequenced so far within  the cdc project  we can also generate clay doughnut  charts to show how the prevalence of  each clade has or hasn't changed in the  entire data set  at the state level obviously there are  some sample size effects here but it's  still interesting  to visualize and a few interesting  things have appeared in the last few  months especially relating to trends of  the variance of concern in some states  and a good example of this is the  evolution of the clade distribution in  florida  which is having a significant outbreak  of the b.117 variant also known  and listed here as 20i 501y  v1 you can see that variant approaching  about  25 of the samples that we see every week  out of florida  but you might be asking yourself what  the work we've done has actually made an  impact and  i think the answer to that question  would be yes it was a lab corp  sequencing surveillance efforts that  detected the first case of the south  african variant in the u.s  proving that we're able to detect things  when we look for them and  we can have an impact on our ability to  combat the virus by  providing information to state and  federal teams as they respond  to the emergence of these new variants  and this isn't the only set of  first cases we've detected after  detecting the 351 variant in south  carolina cdc asked us to focus our  sequencing efforts geographically  and again this proves out the power of  the system we've developed  and the enormous reach that we have  nationally cdc asked us to concentrate  picking to the southeast so we did  and increased sampling across the  southeast from about 100 samples per  state  to an average of about 300.  um and that sampling paid off because we  picked up five  more um 351 samples that week  and then that set we picked up two that  were newly discovered in virginia  and and in illinois following that  focused effort  we have since returned to unbiased  sampling across the u.s and  have seen an increase in the cases of  both b.117 and b.351  obviously the denominator matters here  but this is a  good early evidence that pandemic  surveillance does  actually allow us to capture variants as  they spread across the u.s  more recently there have been reports of  a new variant of  b.1.526 that was first identified in new  york  this variant uh includes the e484 k  spike mutation which  resides in the receptor binding domain  um then we saw the first of  these cases on february 6th in new york  and connecticut  and over the next two weeks we saw the  case load increase to about 179  primarily in the northeast with a few  emerging in the southeast in north  carolina and georgia  b.1.526 now represents about 10 to 20  percent of the cases that we're seeing  come out of new york and new jersey  and watching variants like this through  genomic surveillance is important  um and hopefully with the renewed  interest in  and science-based policy we and others  are are further enabled to continue this  work and  sequencing thousands of samples a week  and  and 526 itself represents an interesting  case because new york and new jersey had  a  pretty big outbreak of stars kobe 2  back around this time last year so the  the idea would be that they have built  up a reasonable  amount of population immunity and it  appears that  this virus doesn't really care about  that um  but one of the most important aspects of  the  labcorp data set is that it's based on  random sampling unlike other sequencing  initiatives that target sequencing  samples that they believe are variants  of concern we sequence samples randomly  across the nation  this limits the level of sampling bias  in our data set although there  are still states that we end up sampling  more than others  but generally this data set is much less  targeted than other initiatives  because of this we can look at national  trends and the distribution of clades  across the country  the dominant clade throughout our  sampling has been 20g  which is the major u.s clade that was  described early on in the pandemic  in the united states but as you can see  here in the last few weeks the  prevalence of 20g  has been reduced while 20i501y.v1 which  is b.117  and clade 20b are seeing a bit of an  uptick  indicating that the other two clades may  be out competing 20g  potentially a little or too early to to  say that but that that looks like that's  where the the  the trend is going um and this trend um  for b117 is  also in agreement with early predictions  that this variant would become the  major u.s variant by by the beginning to  the end of march  so i hope that this presentation has  helped convince you  of the importance of genomic  surveillance during a pandemic  sequencing based surveillance allows us  to know the things that we don't know  uh we've deposited over 15 000 sequences  and you say it over the  past nine weeks in collaboration with  cdc combined with the work of other  state labs and universities  approximately 10 000 new sequences are  making it into the databases every week  and and with that we're finally now  getting the data and the tools we need  to respond to this pandemic with a  reasoned scientific approach and here  i'm showing a  a 